BAB II
PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES, AND SERVICE PRINCIPLES
Article 2
Protection of women and children victims of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. Respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Non-discrimination;
d. Best interests of victims.

CHAPTER X
INVESTIGATION
Article 18
(1) Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regional Government are given special authority as investigators to conduct investigations of criminal acts in the field of protection of women and children as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Law.
(2) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) are certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regional Government who are appointed by authorized Officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) The authority of Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is:
a. to receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of a criminal act;
b. to take first action and examination at the scene;
c. to order a person to stop and examine the identity of the suspect;
d. to conduct seizure of objects and documents;
e. to take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. to summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. to bring in experts needed in connection with the examination of the case;
h. to conduct termination of investigation after receiving instructions from the investigator of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia that there is insufficient evidence or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the investigator notify this matter to the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family.

Article 9
The obligations and responsibilities of the community are as follows:
a. making efforts to provide support for the development of violence prevention programs;
b. preparing proposals regarding the formulation and policies on protection;
c. making...
c. making efforts to provide protection and moral or material support to victims;
d. conducting supervision and reporting of the occurrence of acts of violence to the competent authorities.

CHAPTER VII
FUNDING
Article 15
(1) Funding for the implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, and medication costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government, and is conducted transparently and accounted for in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
(3) Sources of funds in the implementation of protection for women and children are sourced from the State Revenue and Expenditure Budget, the Provincial Revenue and Expenditure Budget, the Sintang Regency APBD as well as other sources that are legal and non-binding.

CHAPTER VI
ASSISTANCE
Article 14
(1) Assistance to victims is carried out by, among others:
a. Police;
b. Prosecutor's Office;
c. Advocates; and
d. Social Workers.
(2) Assistance by the Police, Prosecutor's Office, and Advocates as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, letter b, and letter c, shall be in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.
(3) The duties of social workers as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d as assistants are as follows:
a. providing sufficient information to victims about their rights;
b. providing psychosocial services to victims so that victims feel safe and comfortable;
c. assisting victims during the examination and medical recovery process;
d. assisting victims during the medicolegal process;
e. assisting victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office, and Court;
f. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office, and Court;
g. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;
h. coordinating with other assistants;
i. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 6
Every victim has the obligation to:
a. not take revenge against the perpetrator of violence;
b. become a witness if it does not endanger oneself and if there is a security guarantee for the victim;
c. give the perpetrator an opportunity to provide compensation to the victim in addition to the punishment determined by the Court;
d. be willing to obtain assistance;
e. together with the community prevent the increase of other victims of violence.

Article 12
Forms of services for victims include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services, which are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services, which are services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength, and independence in resolving their problems;
d. Legal services, which are services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. Economic independence services, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent;
f. Information Services (Informed Consent), related to services and assistance.

